## The operator's gut check

The line I would actually say at the bar:

> **"Sure, we can build a quiet airport. Every quiet airport I have seen is quiet because somebody hired an extra 200 human beings in vests standing at decision points. Copenhagen is not quiet because they turned off the PA. Copenhagen is quiet because when you look confused, somebody walks up to you. If we do not fund the ambassador program, the quiet standard is just a way to make it the passenger's fault when they miss the flight."**

Everything else is dressing. Every operator I know who has toured Schiphol or Copenhagen or Changi and come home wanting to build a quiet airport at their place has quietly discovered that the labor budget to run one is 30-40% higher on the passenger-facing floor, and their CFO does not want to hear it. **MWAA's Traveler Assistance program at IAD is thin. At DCA it is thinner. If the standard does not obligate a staffing model — not just a tech spec — it will fail the first bad-weather Friday.** And when it fails, the airline will point at the standard and the standard will point at the airline and the passenger will write to their Congressman.

The other thing I would say at the bar: **whoever writes the exceptions process controls the standard.** The exception is where all the interesting fights are — TSA scripts that MWAA cannot override, airline hub-specific paging language, tenant PA rights in club lounges, emergency management overrides, ADA-mandated content. If the standard does not name who owns the exception and how fast the exception can be granted, every stakeholder will file an exception and the standard will be a decorative document within eighteen months. Ask me how I know.

