## Productive tangents

**Tangents that turned out to matter:**

- **Hospitals and HCAHPS.** Started as a "how does everybody else do quiet" side trip; ended up being the sharpest analogy in the brief. The shared-auditory-field-as-coordination-signal argument is directly transferable, and the empirical alarm-fatigue data is stronger than anything the airport industry has published on its own quiet initiatives. Worth citing in Stage 2.
- **Frankfurt Auracast pilot (February 2026).** This is *live* and this is *close* — Fraport announced it in January 2026 and it is running now. Whatever MWAA writes will be judged against Frankfurt's early results within eighteen months. Any standard that does not have an Auracast/BLE broadcast provisioning clause will be embarrassingly out of date on day one. That said — the two-tier passenger experience is a real accessibility risk and the Frankfurt press release is not being honest about it.
- **Moynihan vs. Grand Central.** Nobody in aviation is making this comparison and they should. Transit terminals are the closest architectural analog for a large concourse and Moynihan is the freshest, most-photographed, most-praised design of the last decade. It is also, operationally, a step backward. That is a *real* cautionary tale for a Board about to spend $22.5B at IAD.

**Tangents I went down that did not pan out — but leaving in because the Strategist might see a use:**

- **Casinos and cinema-quiet retail.** I chased whether the "no clocks, low ambient music" casino model has anything to teach an airport concessions floor. The parallel is real (dwell time responds to ambient sound), but I could not find an airport-relevant dataset on concession spend vs. ambient sound level. Leaving the pointer here in case Infrastructure-Economist has run this analysis independently. If dwell-time behavior is a meaningful revenue variable, the quiet standard has a concessions-revenue side effect the Board should be told about.
- **The 2025 DCA mid-air collision and its comms-standards implications.** I started chasing whether the January 29, 2025 collision led to any PA-content changes (helicopter-route NOTAM messaging, TFR notifications) and whether that constrains DCA-side design assumptions. The public trail is thin. Flagging as an evidence gap; the emergency-management director agent probably has this and I should not duplicate.

